19
00:01:45,630 --> 00:01:49,750
To get started, can you give us a brief history of JavaScript and how it came about and how it evolved?

20
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:51,910
Absolutely.

21
00:01:51,910 --> 00:01:59,070
So, I think most people listening will know that JavaScript started from a 1995 era, created in a very short period of time,

22
00:01:59,070 --> 00:02:11,510
something like 10 days, supposedly, and never really was expected to be the home of all modern dynamic web applications that it's become.

23
00:02:11,510 --> 00:02:19,270
By the early 2000s, creative folk behind web browsers were starting to realize the limit of plugins,

24
00:02:19,270 --> 00:02:28,870
so things like Java applets that were the at that point default, or Flash, that were the default techniques for adding interactivity to a web application,

25
00:02:28,870 --> 00:02:34,190
and started pushing the limits of JavaScript, both with existing features and starting to introduce new features.

26
00:02:34,190 --> 00:02:44,590
So, one of the pioneers of that, of course, is Google Maps, as I'm sure, again, many of your listeners will know, may even have a memory of first interacting with the web browser, a pretty amazing moment, I think.

27
00:02:44,590 --> 00:02:48,910
My first light bulb moment was with a site called Netvibes.

28
00:02:48,910 --> 00:02:54,510
Sure, almost nobody remembers it, but it was a dynamic home page with, again, draggable components, something that we now take for granted,

29
00:02:54,510 --> 00:03:05,190
but back in 2004 was a really significant moment in what you could imagine could be built within this environment, the web browser.

30
00:03:05,190 --> 00:03:10,150
Fast forward to today, and the language JavaScript is now a much more mature language.

31
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:19,350
That's a combination of the TC39 committee that has responded to needs from the community for nearly decades now of being responsive to the community and adding features,

32
00:03:19,350 --> 00:03:22,750
but it's also a coherence of browsers.

33
00:03:22,750 --> 00:03:30,430
So, browser developers backed by pretty substantial organizations working incredibly hard to

34
00:03:30,430 --> 00:03:37,470
evolve their architecture under the hood to make sure that even as huge amount of features have been added,

35
00:03:37,470 --> 00:03:45,590
and web applications have become far heavier in the workloads they're handling, the browser is still able to handle that, and has the capacity to handle it,

36
00:03:45,590 --> 00:03:50,790
both the architectural decisions around it, but also the features available to developers in JavaScript.

37
00:03:50,790 --> 00:03:54,150
And that's not just JavaScript, it's also web browser APIs that are part of the experience.

38
00:03:54,150 --> 00:04:02,670
I think a lot of us might sometimes get confused what is JavaScript and what are web browser APIs,

39
00:04:02,670 --> 00:04:12,670
things like fetch or the access to the camera or local storage, all being essentially browser features and not developed by the ECMA committee, that's the group that works on JavaScript itself.

40
00:04:12,670 --> 00:04:17,190
But they've all started to play hand in hand together much more.

41
00:04:17,190 --> 00:04:25,750
So, while today many people write some abstraction level above, maybe TypeScript, maybe React, at the core JavaScript is now a fairly mature and stable language.

42
00:04:25,750 --> 00:04:33,790
What we can talk about is its additive nature and append-only system because while a Python or

43
00:04:33,790 --> 00:04:45,270
a Java might have a version release cycle where at some point a previous version is deprecated, JavaScript within the browser needs to be backwards compatible within reason all the way back to 1995.

44
00:04:45,270 --> 00:04:53,030
And so that's the constraint in which this language has always been evolving and I think we'll probably talk about some of the decisions that have been made that are a result of that.

45
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:55,510
100%.

46
00:04:55,510 --> 00:05:03,350
And I guess that constraint as it's called don't break the web constraint, given that sort of a constraint that exists, what has that led to?

47
00:05:03,350 --> 00:05:07,150
What kind of improvements have the guardians of JavaScript not been able to make because of that constraint?

48
00:05:07,160 --> 00:05:17,950
Yeah, I mean there's probably many we don't realize without looking at the committee notes or the conversations going behind the scenes, but some that really surfaced to public knowledge,

49
00:05:17,950 --> 00:05:21,830
one was 2018 I think,

50
00:05:21,830 --> 00:05:28,590
pretty notorious or not notorious significant moment when the JavaScript developers increasingly recognized that

51
00:05:28,590 --> 00:05:36,470
what we might call user land, so that's libraries developed by the public open source community, so things like Lodash,

52
00:05:36,470 --> 00:05:45,590
had many functions that you would perhaps take for granted in other languages like Python, developed by the community, but not available within JavaScript itself.

53
00:05:45,590 --> 00:05:47,470
So, particularly array methods.

54
00:05:47,470 --> 00:05:58,270
That was around the time when functional paradigm and thinking about data as something to be transformed from function to function without maybe directly mutating became really demanded by the community.

55
00:05:58,270 --> 00:06:07,110
And so, mapping, flattening, type higher-order functions became something that was requested.

56
00:06:07,110 --> 00:06:12,590
And in 2018, it reached the point where a flatten array built-in method was proposed.

57
00:06:12,590 --> 00:06:21,710
Great, sounds good until you realize that there was a MooTools library that was still being used by thousands of applications that

58
00:06:21,710 --> 00:06:29,350
already had a built-in flatten property available to developers to use directly on their arrays through the prototype by mutating it.

59
00:06:29,350 --> 00:06:37,550
And that would now be overridden if a flatten method was added at the language primitive level, the standard library level.

60
00:06:37,550 --> 00:06:44,030
And so, after much debate, including potentially calling it smoosh, which would have been much better name.

61
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:48,110
Exactly. And they came up with smooshgate.

62
00:06:48,110 --> 00:06:50,350
The agreement came to create a flat array method.

63
00:06:50,350 --> 00:06:57,990
And when you think about wanting your method names to reflect their action, flatten is more appropriate.

64
00:06:57,990 --> 00:07:07,070
But in the end, flat was chosen because the legacy of previous libraries that still needed to be compatible with restricted what was really available.

65
00:07:07,070 --> 00:07:11,310
And that is the again guiding principle of JavaScript developers.

66
00:07:11,310 --> 00:07:20,790
So, I think one of the most significant steps the language creators took was introducing therefore an entirely new primitive known as a symbol.

67
00:07:20,790 --> 00:07:27,110
And I actually did a new version of the hard parts of JavaScript a few months ago and did a whole section on symbols.

68
00:07:27,110 --> 00:07:33,750
They're one of the most counterintuitive constructs that you could possibly imagine until you realize that

69
00:07:33,750 --> 00:07:40,710
they are designed essentially as a workaround for not adding new properties to objects where there is a chance of collision.

70
00:07:40,710 --> 00:07:51,470
And I don't mean collision in the hash map sense, but just collision of a name within JavaScript itself with existing libraries or existing developers custom properties [clears throat] added to an object.

71
00:07:51,480 --> 00:08:06,070
Meaning that if you are a developer and you've written code that's going to loop through all your publicly available properties on your object and suddenly there's a new property added by JavaScript's core team, then suddenly you're going to be displaying a lot more than you expected on the UI.

72
00:08:06,070 --> 00:08:11,230
And so a new primitive was introduced, the symbol,

73
00:08:11,230 --> 00:08:21,190
that gives JavaScript and then also developers through meta programming techniques the ability to add new features to objects without the risk of clashing on existing properties.

74
00:08:21,190 --> 00:08:25,470
And this is the result of having a language that needs to be backwards compatible.

75
00:08:25,480 --> 00:08:28,430
There ain't no this is 2.7.

76
00:08:28,430 --> 00:08:37,630
If you're using it, you need to use the existing features, but if you want to use the new features, you need to accept the entire code base will be deprecated unless you upgrade to the new version.

77
00:08:37,630 --> 00:08:39,430
Doesn't exist in JavaScript.

78
00:08:39,430 --> 00:08:49,510
Fascinating constraint has led to things like symbols that allow you to add properties that are only accessible through certain entry points within the code,

79
00:08:49,510 --> 00:08:59,670
meaning that the change in ability, the change in behavior of JavaScript is possible, but not without conscious decision by developer to use it.

80
00:08:59,670 --> 00:09:01,790
No unconscious accidental interaction with it.

81
00:09:01,800 --> 00:09:12,750
In terms of the constraint, is it right for me to summarize that right at the beginning you had a lot of userland libraries like Lodash, like Moment, that do fairly standard things like formatting time,

82
00:09:12,750 --> 00:09:14,750
being able to format strings and so forth,

83
00:09:14,750 --> 00:09:27,310
which people have gotten so used to that it's impossible for TC39 to introduce these fairly standard features into the language without essentially forcing people to stop using those libraries. Is that accurate?

84
00:09:27,320 --> 00:09:30,190
I think it's accurate.

85
00:09:30,190 --> 00:09:40,230
I think at this point everybody in development is excited by the new built-in temporal feature

86
00:09:40,230 --> 00:09:45,870
of JavaScript that will allow a more modern interface for time because the moment library, while there's lightweight versions,

87
00:09:45,870 --> 00:09:56,270
is quite a heavy library just for access to time features that work as you might expect that tie to the ISO standards for thinking about time.

88
00:09:56,270 --> 00:10:03,790
And so, yeah, the changes still get made, but the day that all developers move off moment and use the temporal object directly,

89
00:10:03,790 --> 00:10:09,270
I mean, that'll be a surprise because your code base, I mean, this is what we'll talk about later,

90
00:10:09,270 --> 00:10:19,950
I'm sure, but the individual JavaScript features that you might benefit from switching to new vanilla features, new built-in standard library features, is a question less of performance and more of maintenance.

91
00:10:19,950 --> 00:10:31,270
The gains to maintenance are potentially significant, but the legacy dependencies of these libraries may sit across millions of lines of code, and so switching out is no small feat.

92
00:10:31,280 --> 00:10:34,710
Agree.

93
00:10:34,710 --> 00:10:38,870
Now, what actually led TC39 to start absorbing these userland patterns into the language?

94
00:10:38,870 --> 00:10:44,790
Was it mainly driven by performance, security concerns, or was it a case of it's high time we do this now or never?

95
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:46,630
Yeah, that's a great question.

96
00:10:46,630 --> 00:10:54,150
So, I think we can agree that JavaScript's standard library is still surprisingly thin.

97
00:10:54,150 --> 00:11:02,470
So, engineers still struggle with, or did until very recently, one of the most is deep cloning or immutable objects,

98
00:11:02,470 --> 00:11:09,510
and I think the hack has been the JSON.parse stringify step that maybe we're all familiar with, but that's a performance hit, undoubtedly.

99
00:11:09,510 --> 00:11:15,670
And that comes again from this concern, do not break the web, this defining principle that they've experienced for so long.

100
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:23,110
However, because the library is so thin, the community has developed great tools to fix that gap.

101
00:11:23,110 --> 00:11:30,710
I think the way that TC39 thinks about it is if the community develops almost as an experimental approach,

102
00:11:30,710 --> 00:11:36,790
then at a certain point, we see the demand is significant enough that we then add and develop that feature ourselves.

103
00:11:36,790 --> 00:11:43,470
So, I think in some cases it's performance with deep cloning, I think we've got finely structured clone,

104
00:11:43,470 --> 00:11:51,670
but that is definitely to overcome the performance hit of using either a low dash library, which is perhaps less of a performance hit or more likely a JSON parse JSON.stringify.

105
00:11:51,680 --> 00:11:56,510
For others, I think it's a responsiveness to the community.

106
00:11:56,510 --> 00:12:02,070
Let it be fought out in almost user land as R&amp;D.

107
00:12:02,070 --> 00:12:04,950
And then TC39 absorbs the patterns once the community proves they're essential.

108
00:12:04,950 --> 00:12:08,190
It's almost a filtration system.

109
00:12:08,190 --> 00:12:15,190
So, I think performance is important, but also if you are a language that is used so broadly in so many different domains,

110
00:12:15,190 --> 00:12:23,790
not just the browser of course, but node and bun and everywhere else, you can use your community and the development of their own libraries as a filtration system.

111
00:12:23,800 --> 00:12:28,430
What really emerges as the standard universal need?

112
00:12:28,430 --> 00:12:30,910
Okay, it's been around for long enough, now we integrate it.

113
00:12:30,920 --> 00:12:32,870
That makes a lot of sense.

114
00:12:32,870 --> 00:12:34,829
Do they end up waiting for too long though?

115
00:12:34,829 --> 00:12:41,750
So, something new came up, everyone started using it, and you've waited so long that people are just going to be resistant to switch to anything new.

116
00:12:41,760 --> 00:12:46,390
I think the answer is definitely yes before 2015.

117
00:12:46,390 --> 00:12:49,110
I think since 2015, the release cycle is much more reasonable.

118
00:12:49,120 --> 00:12:59,590
And now, I think it's fair to say that you actually see features shaped at a sustainable rate where there is enough awareness that

119
00:12:59,590 --> 00:13:06,430
you're what we're calling robotics the end effector, in this case the browser or the node environment, they also have changes.

120
00:13:06,430 --> 00:13:08,910
So, the needs come and go.

121
00:13:08,910 --> 00:13:15,190
So, patience around that is a suitable thing when you're defining a standard library that is used in so many different end effectors.

122
00:13:15,190 --> 00:13:17,870
But equally, there needs to be a steady regular pace.

123
00:13:17,880 --> 00:13:21,750
It was only in 2015 that that really started.

124
00:13:21,750 --> 00:13:24,110
You started getting yearly releases.

125
00:13:24,110 --> 00:13:31,590
Until 2015, if there were yearly releases, they they thought about as a mature cycle, and 2015 was when I think the shift really happened.

126
00:13:31,590 --> 00:13:40,870
So, what you describe as the slow pace, I think that's really something that's no longer an issue, but it really was before ES2015, for sure.

127
00:13:40,880 --> 00:13:43,830
Makes sense. And thank you for clarifying that.

128
00:13:43,830 --> 00:13:50,870
As an instructor who sees thousands of developers, do you find folks eager to try out these new features,

129
00:13:50,870 --> 00:13:58,110
or is there a tendency to go with what works because there's so much code out there that still uses the user land libraries to discontinue using that and not try out the new stuff?

130
00:13:58,120 --> 00:14:07,070
I think the former when people are really looking for a performance upgrade.

131
00:14:07,070 --> 00:14:17,030
Where a feature adds a serious performance upgrade, things like structured clone, things like the temporal object, for want of a better term, when it's released a temporal feature, I think you see that.

132
00:14:17,040 --> 00:14:31,310
Performance gains are so often, within the given language itself, secondary to all the other performance killers that step ahead of unoptimized JavaScript, right?

133
00:14:31,310 --> 00:14:42,190
So, you know, I talk to engineers in the team, but obviously alums of CodeSmith working enormous code bases, and far more of an issue for them are things like unoptimized fetch requests that pull an entire example.

134
00:14:42,190 --> 00:14:51,190
I was talking to an engineer who works at a sports data company, and they pull a single player, and they get their entire team history.

135
00:14:51,190 --> 00:14:53,070
They get their entire game history.

136
00:14:53,070 --> 00:15:00,990
They get their entire interaction with other players in different games, and that's the API available.

137
00:15:00,990 --> 00:15:11,670
And so, far more often, the pressure is to fix the fetch than it is to improve an edge case performance gain that comes from switching from a Lodash to a pure JavaScript feature.

138
00:15:11,670 --> 00:15:17,270
There are exceptions to that, of course, but in terms of performance, I think it tends to be secondary.

139
00:15:17,270 --> 00:15:20,390
Now, where I think there's a lot of interest, of course, is library creators.

140
00:15:20,390 --> 00:15:32,190
So, when you're a library creator, the ability to use native features that are more performant and easier to write and easier to reason with, that's where you start to see a change.

141
00:15:32,190 --> 00:15:39,310
And I think you see that also over years when some of the features built in are super similar to other languages.

142
00:15:39,310 --> 00:15:45,390
So, I think the await feature is one of the most broadly adopted feature of JavaScript.

143
00:15:45,390 --> 00:15:49,270
And not a surprise that it's a feature that is very analogous to other languages.

144
00:15:49,270 --> 00:15:57,270
And also a feature that reverts the order of control to give the developer at the very least a sense that

145
00:15:57,270 --> 00:16:05,030
the code is executing line by line down the page and not one of the hardest things to reason about,

146
00:16:05,030 --> 00:16:13,350
which is a function definition being passed to a function call and for you to understand as a developer that somehow that function definition is not going to be executed until

147
00:16:13,350 --> 00:16:20,750
some point at a later stage when some actions in my own happens and only then is that function called.

148
00:16:20,750 --> 00:16:27,630
And by the way, you better have any work you want to happen on that call be inside that function definition.

149
00:16:27,630 --> 00:16:31,070
That is the most challenging mental model for people to get their heads around.

150
00:16:31,070 --> 00:16:41,310
Whereas, once you introduce an await keyword, it's code linearly down the page until you hit await, do the async task, get the result back, and then code continues down the page from there.

151
00:16:41,310 --> 00:16:45,350
The problem is, of course, that does not reflect the actual execution pattern.

152
00:16:45,350 --> 00:16:51,510
If that await is within a function, an async function, then the code outside that async function's call will continue.

153
00:16:51,510 --> 00:16:58,190
And your awaited code will not execute until later until all global code's finished executing.

154
00:16:58,190 --> 00:17:09,270
So, yeah, that's the problem when you have really appealing abstractions, really appealing new features, and when they're not understood, there's a huge disconnect between how it really works and maybe what it looks like on the page.

155
00:17:09,270 --> 00:17:17,550
And that's when you get those bugs that unless you have under the hood understanding, and that's my passion of mine is to give people that under the hood understanding, you're not going to be able to debug.

156
00:17:17,560 --> 00:17:32,910
Makes a lot of sense. I think it's useful for listeners to also understand JavaScript as a language, it's always been the situation where you can write whatever you want, and it's the job of the engine to fix it, to essentially save your bad code. And that's shifting now, right?

157
00:17:32,910 --> 00:17:36,870
It will help to sort of understand why this tendency to save bad code exists within JavaScript.

158
00:17:36,880 --> 00:17:39,830
Yeah, and it is shifting.

159
00:17:39,830 --> 00:17:47,350
So, I think from the very start, there was an inclination to have JavaScript be a very flexible language.

160
00:17:47,350 --> 00:17:49,470
There's a couple of reasons for that.

161
00:17:49,470 --> 00:18:00,071
One, I think it was a scripting language in theory for people who were primarily thinking about the pixels on the page or communicating text on the page to be able to add a little bit of dynamism. That's it.

162
00:18:00,071 --> 00:18:01,910
That was its goal.

163
00:18:01,910 --> 00:18:04,630
So, from the start, it had a very flexible structure because it's a scripting language.

164
00:18:04,630 --> 00:18:09,110
It's not designed to be a robust end-to-end production application development language.

165
00:18:09,110 --> 00:18:12,190
Now, obviously, that's no longer the case, even close, but it was where it started.

166
00:18:12,200 --> 00:18:23,350
Secondly, JavaScript's most typical environment in which it runs, the web browser, is a messy set of APIs for interacting with the page,

167
00:18:23,350 --> 00:18:30,190
the DOM, the document, for other features, text, HTTP messages from the network.

168
00:18:30,190 --> 00:18:39,230
These are all external interfaces that again, from early on, were across many different browsers implemented in many different ways.

169
00:18:39,230 --> 00:18:49,590
And so, the hope was that if you let JavaScript be flexible, that including, by the way, automatically coercing types, data types from one to another,

170
00:18:49,590 --> 00:19:01,790
but at that interface between the JavaScript land and the web browser, the DOM, the network, that maybe JavaScript could automatically help you coerce things that came off the web browser as text strings into numbers.

171
00:19:01,800 --> 00:19:05,910
And, of course, in theory, in a small scale, that might be quite helpful.

172
00:19:05,910 --> 00:19:11,910
In practice, at real application scale, that becomes extremely difficult and dangerous.

173
00:19:11,910 --> 00:19:20,070
And that's where TypeScript and or more generally type checking at every boundary between JavaScript and any IO becomes vital.

174
00:19:20,070 --> 00:19:25,510
But the underlying principle was coming from a relatively informed place.

175
00:19:25,510 --> 00:19:30,310
It just isn't suited to this type of using that language as it is now for complex applications.

176
00:19:30,320 --> 00:19:33,630
And I guess that having it flexible really helped in its growth, right?

177
00:19:33,630 --> 00:19:41,190
The fact that pretty much 100% of the web is based on JavaScript or TypeScript is testament to the fact that this was such an easy language.

178
00:19:41,190 --> 00:19:43,870
The entry barrier was pretty low and it was pretty forgiving for the most part.

179
00:19:43,880 --> 00:19:45,870
I think that's right.

180
00:19:45,870 --> 00:19:51,430
Forgiving in the sense that your error is handed to the user, right?

181
00:19:51,430 --> 00:19:53,990
As opposed to at a compile time stage.

182
00:19:53,990 --> 00:19:59,830
I think it's also gave it a really rough reputation.

183
00:19:59,830 --> 00:20:01,990
My sense would be that it's almost we call it syntactic sugar.

184
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:07,950
Syntactic sugar is really dangerous because it can create this artificial sense of understanding.

185
00:20:07,950 --> 00:20:13,430
I'm able to technically write it and follow it, but as soon as you hit a more complex edge case, let's think about the event loop.

186
00:20:13,440 --> 00:20:22,591
The event loop is a actually fairly clear model once it's explicitly defined. And yet,

187
00:20:22,591 --> 00:20:28,150
if you only see the JavaScript runtime and you only see your code technically not erroring because

188
00:20:28,150 --> 00:20:35,670
an undefined returned or an undefined value doesn't cause a compile error, then you might think it's working.

189
00:20:35,670 --> 00:20:42,350
Or you might at the very least not hit the block and feel the error and then try another tool.

190
00:20:42,350 --> 00:20:49,150
But in practice, until you have that mental model of the event loop, the callback queue, the call stack, you're really flying blind.

191
00:20:49,150 --> 00:20:53,110
Once you have them though, I do think JavaScript with the flexibility is really appealing.

192
00:20:53,110 --> 00:21:03,070
If you have a really strong mental model of this language, and it's why I have taught my workshops on it for years and I long ago switched to teaching neural networks and AI model,

193
00:21:03,070 --> 00:21:10,350
but I still come back to teaching JavaScript because you have a a of the core principles of programming languages implemented in JavaScript.

194
00:21:10,350 --> 00:21:17,750
You have an event loop, you have an asynchronous IO design, you have queuing, you have a way of at least thinking about inheritance.

195
00:21:17,750 --> 00:21:23,030
It's not native classical inheritance, but you have a way of thinking about it with a prototype chain.

196
00:21:23,030 --> 00:21:28,590
You have flexible and interesting ways of thinking about persisting data between different function calls.

197
00:21:28,590 --> 00:21:34,190
You have closure to do that, or you have other patterns you can use.

198
00:21:34,190 --> 00:21:36,230
And the variety actually does become really appealing as you say.

199
00:21:36,230 --> 00:21:43,110
The flexibility becomes really appealing, but I would just strongly say only once you have a clear mental model.

200
00:21:43,120 --> 00:21:51,750
Otherwise, you hear people being very critical of JavaScript because the flexibility where you don't understand under the hood is a curse.

201
00:21:51,750 --> 00:21:55,150
But when you do understand under the hood, it becomes an asset.

