00:22:20,350 --> 00:22:24,270
I think it's a huge edge, but only built on core fundamentals.

208
00:22:24,280 --> 00:22:28,510
Another thing that you mentioned that I just want to make sure our listeners understand, syntactic sugar.

209
00:22:28,510 --> 00:22:31,510
What does syntactic sugar mean in the context of JavaScript?

210
00:22:31,520 --> 00:22:36,390
Yeah, a perfect example would be OOP, object-oriented programming.

211
00:22:36,390 --> 00:22:41,550
So, this is for many the essential design pattern for building complex applications.

212
00:22:41,560 --> 00:22:53,110
Allows you to think about things in a structured way, to think about properties that are universal, properties that belong to the subclasses, data that is shared between different features, data that is not.

213
00:22:53,120 --> 00:22:56,790
It's an extraordinary way to structure a complex application.

214
00:22:56,790 --> 00:23:08,070
Okay, JavaScript lets you do that, and it does it using a architecture under the hood that is absolutely not classical inheritance, not classical object-oriented programming.

215
00:23:08,070 --> 00:23:17,350
Instead, it uses a feature called the prototype chain that allows objects to access, I wouldn't even call it inherit, but access functions and features of other objects down a chain.

216
00:23:17,360 --> 00:23:27,150
However, on the surface, the keywords used, words like new or class, they are very similar,

217
00:23:27,150 --> 00:23:36,590
they are identical to the keywords used in natively object-oriented languages that don't use a chain model under the hood that gives objects access to other functions,

218
00:23:36,590 --> 00:23:42,150
but not direct inheritance as is built into natively OOP languages.

219
00:23:42,150 --> 00:23:46,510
That creates a lot of problems cuz when you use a new keyword,

220
00:23:46,510 --> 00:23:55,910
if you're not aware that under the hood it's creating an object inside of the function you're calling with the new keyword, it's adding any properties you add to it using the keyword this is access,

221
00:23:55,910 --> 00:24:06,550
it's automatically returning that object out, but by the way, if you call a function without the new keyword, it's going to attach all those properties to the native global object, then you're going to look at JavaScript and go, "What the heck?

222
00:24:06,550 --> 00:24:08,950
This doesn't mirror anything that I know about OOP."

223
00:24:08,960 --> 00:24:13,590
And so, your syntactic sugar tricks you into thinking it's native OOP.

224
00:24:13,600 --> 00:24:16,870
Essentially, it's a little bit of an illusion.

225
00:24:16,870 --> 00:24:19,350
So, even though it looks like OOP, it's not actually that.

226
00:24:19,350 --> 00:24:25,910
And you can continue using it that way, but if you understand how the internals work, you can actually get the full benefit of the way it's designed.

227
00:24:25,920 --> 00:24:29,830
Correct? Full benefit, but also have it not do things you don't expect.

228
00:24:29,830 --> 00:24:36,790
So, in a classical OOP language, you're not able to use a constructor function without the new keyword.

229
00:24:36,790 --> 00:24:45,350
In JavaScript, you could use the very same function you expect to be constructing an object without a new keyword, and it will create a new set of properties that you've passed to that function,

230
00:24:45,350 --> 00:24:51,470
but unfortunately, they'll be attached to the default global object that's available in JavaScript,

231
00:24:51,470 --> 00:24:59,590
and you won't get an object returned out because the new keyword will automate the process of creating an object, adding properties, and returning it out.

232
00:24:59,590 --> 00:25:04,630
The idea that that is being done not as a base level feature,

233
00:25:04,640 --> 00:25:15,870
but as a syntactic sugar that's then manually creating your object under the hood, meaning you could still run that function without the new keyword, and it will try to add properties to its default object,

234
00:25:15,870 --> 00:25:20,390
not the object you want it to add, that creates a lot of frustration in engineers.

235
00:25:20,390 --> 00:25:28,310
One of the key questions that Google asked of any engineer working with JavaScript, their favorite question to ask was what's the new keyword doing under the hood?

236
00:25:28,320 --> 00:25:36,990
Because they know that that is, along with how's closure work, essentially the kind of tell me that you really understand how this system is working.

237
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:39,070
Makes sense.

238
00:25:39,070 --> 00:25:41,790
Let's switch gears a little bit because we are I think we are halfway through the interview now.

239
00:25:41,790 --> 00:25:45,390
When we talk about JavaScript, the word engine gets bandied about a lot.

240
00:25:45,390 --> 00:25:53,710
When JavaScript engineers talk about engine, be it Google V8, be it Apple's JavaScript core, SpiderMonkey, what are they actually referring to in terms of the stack?

241
00:25:53,710 --> 00:25:55,510
Is it a virtual machine, a compiler, or something else?

242
00:25:55,520 --> 00:25:57,150
It can be a number of things.

243
00:25:57,150 --> 00:26:04,510
I think one thing to recognize is it is not the entire web browser.

244
00:26:04,510 --> 00:26:10,070
It is a engine that can be separately run in many environments,

245
00:26:10,070 --> 00:26:21,550
both the web browser, but also it can be directly run within your core system as in Node, and therefore has to be supplied with all the other pieces you'd need if you're running on your system directly, like ability to have IO with the network.

246
00:26:21,550 --> 00:26:25,670
If you're in the browser, it needs to be integrated with the browser features.

247
00:26:25,670 --> 00:26:36,390
That's why it's quite hard to pin down exactly what we mean when we speak about an engine because of course a V8 engine from Google is integrated into the Chrome browser.

248
00:26:36,390 --> 00:26:45,870
So, if you're working on the V8 engine, and there are opportunities to optimize directly with the DOM or with the web browser features, you're working closely with the web browser developers.

249
00:26:45,880 --> 00:26:52,430
And so, that's I think why it's quite hard to pin down what people mean exactly when they say engine because the engine doesn't exist in isolation.

250
00:26:52,430 --> 00:26:59,230
In fact, most of what we do in JavaScript is writing code to interface with features outside of the core engine.

251
00:26:59,230 --> 00:27:03,390
And I think that's where a lot of the development has happened as well.

252
00:27:03,390 --> 00:27:10,870
You know, when we switched from XML HTTP request to fetch, that is a web browser feature, but one developed hand-in-hand with the JavaScript engine team.

253
00:27:10,870 --> 00:27:23,630
I think one that's particularly a shift for quite seasoned engineers is the engine has been very helpful at dealing with dynamic code.

254
00:27:23,630 --> 00:27:32,030
So, adding properties to an object at any time and knowing that the engine will probably optimally treat that in a way that is performant.

255
00:27:32,030 --> 00:27:37,270
That can also lead, when those engine changes happen, to anti-patterns.

256
00:27:37,270 --> 00:27:43,390
And I think we're finally, this may be something that your listeners are aware of, within the just-in-time compiler,

257
00:27:43,390 --> 00:27:53,190
which is the backbone of JavaScript engine from V8 from the Google team, the engine now optimizes for what's called monomorphic shapes.

258
00:27:53,190 --> 00:27:59,790
So, that's where your object, as when defined, continues to follow that pattern, that shape, throughout its life cycle.

259
00:27:59,790 --> 00:28:10,710
And adding properties then mid-execution breaks the internal blueprint that's been built at this just-in-time compilation stage and then kills performance.

260
00:28:10,710 --> 00:28:18,950
So, that's the challenge when thinking about engine optimizations is you're firstly working with an engine that is integrated within a browser.

261
00:28:18,950 --> 00:28:24,750
So, even pinning down the exact features of the engine requires you to think about how it interacts with the browser.

262
00:28:24,750 --> 00:28:32,750
But secondly, there are changes only within the last year that throw out what were considered previously senior engineer best practices.

263
00:28:32,750 --> 00:28:40,470
So, I think we're in a moment of change there, and it's going to be interesting to see how well communicated it is.

264
00:28:40,470 --> 00:28:51,870
The JavaScript engine, at least the V8 engine, now allows for optimizations that you can meta-programming style add as comments that give information to the engine on how it should approach the code.

265
00:28:51,870 --> 00:28:57,670
That's something new and I think we're going to see that play out and see how developers pick it up.

266
00:28:57,680 --> 00:29:07,310
Essentially, we're moving to sort of a paradigm where the developer explicitly instructs the engine as opposed to the engine saving back code, which is how it was in the olden days, right?

267
00:29:07,320 --> 00:29:11,470
Correct. Staying with that power engines give you a lot more controls.

268
00:29:11,470 --> 00:29:18,150
Can you give us some examples of the things that developers can do in terms of how you can instruct engine to be optimize one thing or the other?

269
00:29:18,160 --> 00:29:26,950
The main one is consistent monomorphic classes or consistent monomorphic objects.

270
00:29:26,950 --> 00:29:37,390
That is define your object and define on it properties that will be used even if currently null or undefined to give the compiler the pre-programmed shape for the object.

271
00:29:37,390 --> 00:29:45,070
Then do not add properties dynamically afterwards because at that point the blueprint that gets created for the object has to be thrown out.

272
00:29:45,070 --> 00:29:49,790
You know, the sort of the fast rails to accessing that object, you have to switch back to the slow rails.

273
00:29:49,800 --> 00:29:53,390
That's honestly the main one that I'm aware of.

274
00:29:53,400 --> 00:30:01,150
Consistent monomorphic objects, just to make sure I understand, that's essentially a data structure that maintains like a single unchanging shape throughout its life cycle, right?

275
00:30:01,160 --> 00:30:05,550
Correct. Exactly. And having that, what does it change things?

276
00:30:05,550 --> 00:30:07,350
How does it change an app or how does it change the developer's experience?

277
00:30:07,360 --> 00:30:18,350
Honestly, what the V8 team says is that this allows more performant access to object properties at runtime.

278
00:30:18,360 --> 00:30:27,750
What it means for a developer is your standard approach of treating an object as a flexible data structure that you can add or

279
00:30:27,750 --> 00:30:33,670
remove properties from throughout your application's life cycle becomes a sub-optimal approach.

280
00:30:33,670 --> 00:30:39,350
The other features that JavaScript added, I think, that are really interesting that empower developers,

281
00:30:39,360 --> 00:30:51,150
I do think that the symbol adds a whole new set of tools for developers to change how objects, iterators work at the application level.

282
00:30:51,150 --> 00:30:55,910
And so, in the hard parts workshop, I walked through the the symbol principle,

283
00:30:55,910 --> 00:31:04,110
and it allows you to manually override default features of objects, of iterators to follow patterns that you want.

284
00:31:04,110 --> 00:31:14,470
And that's very empowering to engineers, the chance to at the object level across all within the library, change the behavior to your particular target.

285
00:31:14,470 --> 00:31:21,550
That can be anything from as trivial as logging an object and having your own description rather than object object,

286
00:31:21,550 --> 00:31:29,510
instead having your own description that is excellent for debugging, all the way through to manually changing how iterators,

287
00:31:29,510 --> 00:31:35,510
you know, a native feature of JavaScript, iterate over an object or iterate over a set of tasks.

288
00:31:35,510 --> 00:31:45,950
And that metaprogramming ability, where you're changing the behavior of JavaScript without breaking the system, that's I think a really interesting expansion of what JavaScript does.

